A||N||19||guitar god||Something wrong with Fieros?||||1||
Z||000000||guitar god||01-10-2001||02:55 AM||||I am seriouly considering buying a Fiero, but almost everyone I ask about them says not to get one. Nobody, however, seems to be able to tell me whats wrong with them except the fire problems the 84 was have, but even that was fixed. I think the Fiero sounds like an awesome car, but I am trying to look at it from both perspectives. I know almost everyone here, if not everyone, is a Fiero enthusiast, but please let me know of any problems I should be aware of. Thanx.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Sillhouette.com||01-10-2001||03:20 AM||||Buying my Fiero was the greates decision I made.  There are endless mods for it dare I say more than the import scene.  A cople of thoughts.  Depending on your financial situation stay away from 88 last year productions unless you're willing to put up with the high cost of brakes suspensins hubs that break on all cars but on these model years it's more expensive than the 87 ones.  You will get all sorts of info how the 88 is superior in handling you can make anything handle good with the proper modifications.  <BR>Go for it you'll love it.  <P>Later||||reg||1
Z||000002||DaRkLoRD||01-10-2001||03:20 AM||||Well, even the newest Fieros are almost 13 years old now.. so there will always be more mechanical problems than with a new car.<B> **** </B><BR>I don't know of any major problems, other than the chance of a fire if the engine isn't properly maintained.. there are a few ways to increase the cooling in the engine compartment, such as adding an aftermarket decklid scoop to let more air out, and having the manifolds ceramic coated.<P>The biggest problem with these cars is ignorance among non-Fiero owners.. Fieros have a bad rep with a lot of people, because they simply don't know much about them. They're safe, fun little cars.. anyway, welcome to the forum.    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P><B>(****excluding anything built by Ford....    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> )</B><P>------------------<BR>    <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>    <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A><p>[This message has been edited by DaRkLoRD (edited 01-10-2001).]||||reg||10
Z||000003||Gold-86SE||01-10-2001||09:58 AM||||<BR>The biggest problem with owning a Fiero, is that you want more and more Fieros.  You will find yourself eating, drinking, and sleeping Fieros.<P>The Fiero is a fun car and easy to work on, but they have their quirks.  You have to be willing to work on them and be willing to spend money on them.  It is possible to purchase a Fiero that has all kinds of problems, but with tlc, time, and effort most problems can be repaired.<P>The '84 and '88 Fieros are a breed unto themselves.  In '84 the Fiero was only a 4-cylinder and in some cases people have ghosts in their electrical systems.  In '88 the whole suspension was redesigned, providing a much better handling vehicle - no doubt - but more expensive to repair.  '85 through '87 are the most interchangable when considering the Fiero as a whole.<P>If fuel economy is your bag, get a 4-cylinder 5-speed or if you prefer more power buy a V6. Reliability is there and she will take care of you, if you take care of her.  There is absolutely no driving experience like a Fiero.<BR><P>------------------<BR>84 SE 4.10 4 Speed (Cold and Rain Daily Driver)<BR>85 GT Automatic (Restoration Project)<BR>86 SE 4 Speed (Fair Weather Daily Driver)<BR>87 SC 5 Speed (Not Running, V8 Conversion?)||||reg||1
Z||000004||Oreif||01-10-2001||11:20 AM||||Welcome!<BR>A lot of Fiero problems (noted by non-Fiero owners) are misconceptions. I have an 86 SE that was purchased for just over $1000. I probably spent about $1000 fixing it up to be reliable and safe. Many Items were neglected by the previous owner. Once that was done I drove the car daily for 2 years without any problems. (big key to reliability is doing preventative maintanence) Then came the expensive part, Modifying the car my way for performance and handling. There is a large aftermarket out there to do basically whatever you want. Engine conversions are basically what ever you want. Body kits and different panels are available from multiple sources. I did many "custom" things to my car. (Upgraded brakes, lowered suspension, Added a decklid scoop, minor engine and trans upgrades, restored interior with leather seats, installing a stereo system now)<BR> Most of the cars that were "Factory lemons" are long gone. If you want a project car you can get a Fiero for under $1000. If you want a nice daily driver, check out the mall section of the forum. There are many low/mid mileaged cars that are in excellent shape, for sale by owners who have taken great care of the car. <BR>If any problems do arise, 99.9% of the time someone here has had the problem and knows what need to be done to repair it. (saves $$$ on replacing parts that are not the problem)<BR>You will probably hear many things from non-Fiero people but usually once they have rode in the car or driven one they are hooked. I still have people tell me what a death trap the car is, But there was recently a Fiero hit almost head on by a suburu and the driver is alive. (In the 80's the Fiero was second best in crash tests, Volvo was first.)<BR> <BR>Hope this helps.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||RiceCooker||01-10-2001||11:56 AM||||I just want to agree with what everyone else has said so far. A car's reliabliity is only as good as people have treated it. ANY MAKE/MODEL of car can have a million problems if it's treated like $hit. But the fiero is a great car and it will definitely grow on you. You've come to the right place to find out anything and everything you'll ever want or need to know about fieros.<P>Welcome to the forum "Guitar God"<P>Your real name must be Mike McCready then.<P>Later||||reg||6
Z||000006||stimpy||01-10-2001||02:31 PM||||My main advice would be to be picky when shopping.  Don't settle for sloppy steering or wrn-out suspension. I got lucky in that the first Fiero I went looking for was exceptionally tight and well maintained, but I have seen and driven some dawgs.  I think that buying a Fiero from a forum member, like Mr.Fiero or FieroDave would be an excellent move.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Chris McDonald||01-10-2001||03:14 PM||||I, like many other people here have owned many Fieros.  My first one was a problem child 84 that I actually bought from a woman who refused to pay the repair bill at an auto shop.  Yes, I towed it home with a rope---and many times after that.  For reasons unknown to me, despite the continued break downs, the little car really grew on me.  So much that I bought more of them and eventually learned what makes them tick. <P>Most of the Fieros I have owned came to my house on a tow rope or flat bed truck.  Like the old saying---you get what you pay for.  I have spent more hours than I care to add up changing alternaters, water pumps, starters, fixing/replacing cracked manifolds, leaky valve cover gaskets, warped heads, bad fan relays, rebuilding headlight motors, fuel pump changing, changing rear ball joints, CV goots, fixing oil leaks, and chasing electrical gremlins. Oh yeah, don't forget the a/c compressor/leaky hose replacements. <P>My wife on the other hand has owned only two Fieros.  A near perfect 85 SE 4cyl, then later a low mileage 1988 GT.  Other than new paint jobs, a/c compressors, alternaters, starters, electrical gremlins, and oil leaks, her cars were pretty solid.  I went through twelve to her two.  It is true that you will have some difficulty finding suspension parts for the 88.  I had a terrible time finding affordable front suspension parts to fix her bad wheel bearing hubs.<P>I guess what the others say is true as well regarding the age of the car.  With the exception of cars owned by Fiero enthusiasts, they are really starting to get old and most people have not been very kind to them.  It is making it more difficult to find a good affordable car.  You could easily spend $500-$2,500 on a car an then double that amount bringing it up to a presentable safe condition.  <P>So, I don't have any one good answer for you in your quest.  I suggest you take your time and try to look at many different models before selecting one.  Buy a good set of tools and a repair manual.  Get a good towing policy.  No car is perfect and anything mechanical will eventually break. <P>I have to say one final thing.  I started messing around with these things about 10 years ago.  Anyway, it was not until I sold my V8 Fiero that I really found out about this Forum and the many wonderful people around the world supporting these crazy little cars.  With this kind of support, you will be able to work your way through any problem. <P>Have fun!!!    ||||reg||1
Z||000008||286SE'S||01-10-2001||04:12 PM||||I found the perfect car for you in Riverside, California. A nice 87 GT for $3000.<BR>Take a look:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.recycler.com/asp/AdDetails.asp?iID=9541&sBAC=909&bSQ=0" TARGET=_blank>http://www.recycler.com/asp/AdDetails.asp?iID=9541&sBAC=909&bSQ=0</A><P>or a low mileage 85 for $1800.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.recycler.com/asp/AdDetails.asp?iID=33467&sBAC=909&bSQ=0" TARGET=_blank>http://www.recycler.com/asp/AdDetails.asp?iID=33467&sBAC=909&bSQ=0</A> <p>[This message has been edited by 286SE'S (edited 01-10-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||1FST2M6||01-10-2001||04:29 PM||||how close are you tho these guys... <A HREF="http://www.cwestco.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cwestco.com</A>   they can SHOW you otherwise.. I love my car! No troubles that aren't self-inflicted...<P>welcome to the forum!||||reg||1
Z||000010||Philphine||01-10-2001||04:45 PM||||i've got 4<BR>need i say more.<P><BR>none are parts cars (yet) by the way.||||reg||1
Z||000011||FieroLisa||01-10-2001||05:41 PM||||The most common thing I hear about fieros is: Didn't all those burn up (ha and that's why mine is just fine) You're right about the recall being taken care of. I have seen a couple cars burn up on the side of the road, not one of them was a Fiero.<BR>And then there's: Arent those deathtraps? Well no, I believe they got the best safety rating of their class second only to the Volvo (somone correct me if I'm wrong) As we can see in Anthony Barber's case (another long post on the forum) his life could possibly have saved because he WAS driving a Fiero. I love my car. I've spent just as much fixing up my Tracer and my old Subaru as i have the Fieros. Fixing a used car is inevitable. If ya don't want repairs, buy something on warranty that costs an arm and a leg.<P>------------------<BR>Lisa<BR>86 gold 4 speed GT<BR>86 gold 5 speed SC (Sold!)<BR>Columbus, OH/Greensboro NC<BR>AOL IM: FieroLisa<BR>*As Always, Updated Website*<A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fierolisa/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fierolisa/</A>||||reg||1
Z||000012||RaeQwan||01-10-2001||06:20 PM||||Let me try to correct you lisa, and get it wrong myself I'm sure. but didn't the Fiero tie the volvo for safety in 84? the basic design of the car stayed the same so the safety wise part would be the same. My family is tired of me owning a fiero, cuz thats basicly all I talk about. fiero should mean love, cuz thats what you'll be when ya get one.. in love with it  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/raeqwansig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000013||frontal lobe||01-10-2001||06:36 PM||||It's a fun car for the money you will spend.  Go for it.  If you have some basic mechanical ability, you will be able to find instruction here.  If you don't, be sure you have a local mechanic who you feel is reasonable.<P>The car is about 13 or more years old, so some things will need replacement.  Distributor o-ring seems to be something I see people replacing a lot.  I see people complain about how difficult it is to replace a clutch.  The pop-up headlights seems to be another common repair people wind up doing.  Spark plug replacements are reported to be quite a reach on the v-6.  When you go shopping for one, you are supposed to check under the trunk carpet to look for water rusting out by the wheel wells.  The battery tray often gets rusted out.  Need to check that.<P>Those are some common trouble spots I hear from reading the forum.  I don't know that they would be enough that it would make you not want to get one.  To me, the positives way outweigh the negatives.<P>Personal bias, but the v-6 is pretty snappy in acceleration for the price you pay.||||reg||1
Z||000014||kslish||01-10-2001||06:37 PM||||Lisa, you are right about the crash rating.  I've got the '84 Consumer Reports around somewhere that listed the crash test results.  The Fiero was ranked 2nd right behind the Volvo 240 DL to be exact.<P>I'll scan the article sometime and post it when I have time.<P>The other interesting thing that I remember from the C.R. article was about the Fiero's interior design and how it was really an experimental design.  The early 1980's was well before the age of mass-produced airbags and the feds were just beginning to think about passive restraint requirements (remember those funky automated motorized belts some cars ended up having so they could pass federal regs w/o airbags?)  According to the article, the Fiero's interior was designed so it would pass the 30mph fixed barrier crash test WITHOUT the test dummies being belted in.  This way the Fiero could get by without automatic belts until airbags would appear sometime during the next design cycle (which as we all know of course, never happened).  It also explains why it tested so well with belted in dummies (especially on the passenger side, with the shallow dash design of the Fiero the belts will catch you way before your knees would ever come into contact with the dash or so on).  <P>Damn, it's scary that I've read some of the magazine articles about the Fiero so many times that I know them off by heart now.<P>To keep this on topic.  Buy the Fiero, I'd be willing to bet you'll never regret it.    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Ken S.<BR>'86 SC and '87 GT<p>[This message has been edited by kslish (edited 01-10-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000015||Cheever2||01-10-2001||07:01 PM||||Like Oreif said, drive a good Fiero and you'll see what's so great about them.<P>Like Stimpy said, seriously consider buying one from a Forum member.  Why?  Cause you'll be treated right, told exactly what its condition is, and get help after the sale.<P>I would add: get a GOOD one, especially for your first one.  Spend a little extra, because it will give you a LOT extra fun (bad grammar, I know).<P>Welcome to the Forum.  After you start Fieroing, you'll be spending a lot of time here.  Some folks got addicted to this place even before they bought their first Fiero.<BR> <p>[This message has been edited by Cheever2 (edited 01-10-2001).]||||reg||6
Z||000016||qwikgta||01-10-2001||08:08 PM||||I found this site the other day,  I have spent hours and hours every night since reading and reading over and over.  You will love the Fiero.  I have owned several, I have 2 now. <P>I would pick the best one you can get for your money...I picked up my 88 T-top, coupe, 60K miles, from the original owner for $1200.  The deals are out there...<P>My only advice is try to get a GT.  I loved my 87 GT and I miss all the power options.. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>Rob<P>------------------<BR>QWIKGTA<BR>88 T-Top<BR>robsfieroproject.homestead.com/index.html||||reg||1
Z||000017||AL||01-10-2001||09:40 PM||||welcome to the forum,fieros do grow on you,my first one 86 gt i bought for 900,i knew it had head gasket problems so i took the heads off and had them done, i also did the radiator and had that done.in the 2 yrs i've had it i've done a couple mods to it but, it has not givin me any problems, but that's because i do the maintance on it.it has been very reliable,i just finished purchasing or stealing an indy for 250,i'm in the process of getting that one running,since it was sitting for 6 yrs but all in all go for it you will love it. guitar god?<BR> <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=522257&a=3823750&p=37684518&Sequence=0&res=high">  i beg to differ he he<P>------------------<BR> ||||reg||1
Z||000018||FieroLisa||01-10-2001||09:46 PM||||This is only kind of related to this topic but oh well I feel like venting!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> In class today this extremely talkative and annoying girl just really ticked me off. I was telling the 8 people in the class about how I sold the Fiero for $2,300. She said "Someone PAID that much for a piece of crap?" Ohhhhh man did I get mad...I just looked at her and said "Why are they pieces of crap, just give me one reason" She tried the whole unsafe thing, HA! Got her on that one. She just said "Well my dad used to be a mechanic and he won't go near them" Well Good, one more Fiero for US!<BR><P>------------------<BR>Lisa<BR>86 gold 4 speed GT<BR>86 gold 5 speed SC (Sold!)<BR>Columbus, OH/Greensboro NC<BR>AOL IM: FieroLisa<BR>*As Always, Updated Website*<A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fierolisa/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fierolisa/</A>||||reg||1
Z||000019||guitar god||01-11-2001||03:48 AM||||Thanks every1, you've helped me make my descision. I belive i will be making a Fiero purchase. But sadly, I must finish paying off my '97 Tacoma first (a few months left). I found myself driving that like a sportscar, but somehow it just didnt work. I will let you know when i finally do get it.<P>Thanks again!||||reg||1
